-- title:
--   Finite Cantor–Bendixson point rank in the finite-evidence topology
-- statement:
--   The **finite-evidence topology** on a family $\mathcal X$ has the basic neighborhoods
--   $$U_{L,F}=\{M\in\mathcal X: F\subseteq M\subseteq L\},\qquad L\in\mathcal X,\ F\subseteq L\text{ finite}.$$
--   Set $\mathcal X^{(0)}=\mathcal X$ and let $\mathcal X^{(r+1)}$ be $\mathcal X^{(r)}$ with its isolated points (in the subspace topology) removed. A language $L$ has **Cantor–Bendixson point rank $r$** if $L\in\mathcal X^{(r)}\setminus\mathcal X^{(r+1)}$, and **finite point rank** if it has rank $r$ for some natural number $r$.
--
--   Finite rank is the regime in which Theorem 8.2 gives density $1/2-\varepsilon$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The topology is a `def` on the subtype of members of $\mathcal X$, generated by the sets $U_{L,F}$; $M$ is isolated in $\mathcal X^{(r)}$ when some open set meets $\mathcal X^{(r)}$ exactly in $\{M\}$.
-- source:
--   Kleinberg, Wei, Characterizing Local Breadth in Language Generation, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 f924cbfc309339291b1a61b8504342f4050ece6e4e903dd18b2bf541446f1064), p.3; p.16; Definition 3.1, p.17

import Mathlib

open scoped Topology

namespace LocalBreadth.Supplement

variable {P : Type}

/-- The **finite-evidence topology** on `X` (p.3, p.16): generated by the sets
`U_{L,F} = {M ∈ X : F ⊆ M ⊆ L}` with `L ∈ X` and `F ⊆ L` finite.

**Formalization Note.** A `def` on the subtype `↥X`, used through `IsOpen[finiteEvidenceTopology X]`. -/
@[instance_reducible] def finiteEvidenceTopology (X : Set (Set P)) : TopologicalSpace X :=
  TopologicalSpace.generateFrom
    {U | ∃ L ∈ X, ∃ F : Finset P, (F : Set P) ⊆ L ∧ U = {M : X | (F : Set P) ⊆ M.1 ∧ M.1 ⊆ L}}

/-- The Cantor–Bendixson derived sets (Definition 3.1, p.17): `X^(0) = X`, and `X^(r+1)` is `X^(r)`
with its isolated points removed, a point `M` being isolated in `X^(r)` if some open set meets `X^(r)`
in `{M}` exactly (isolation in the subspace topology). -/
def cbDerived (X : Set (Set P)) : ℕ → Set X
  | 0 => Set.univ
  | r + 1 => {M | M ∈ cbDerived X r ∧
      ¬ ∃ U : Set X, IsOpen[finiteEvidenceTopology X] U ∧ U ∩ cbDerived X r = {M}}

/-- `K` has **finite Cantor–Bendixson point rank** in `X`. Kleinberg, Wei, STOC 2027 submission
(manuscript sha256 f924cbfc…), Definition 3.1, p.17: `K ∈ X` and `K ∈ X^(r) \ X^(r+1)` for some
natural number `r`. -/
def HasFiniteCBRank (X : Set (Set P)) (K : Set P) : Prop :=
  ∃ (hK : K ∈ X) (r : ℕ), (⟨K, hK⟩ : X) ∈ cbDerived X r ∧ (⟨K, hK⟩ : X) ∉ cbDerived X (r + 1)

end LocalBreadth.Supplement


